{"seq":37,"time":"2026-09-14T00:30:30.396643Z","scope":"compare.1/attempt.2","session":"compare.1/attempt.2/writer.1","turn":"compare.1/attempt.2/writer.1/turn.1","event":{"type":"session.text.ended","id":"evt_Y29tcGFyZS4xL2F0dGVtcHQuMi93cml0ZXIuMQ_00000000000000000037","created":1789345830396,"data":{"assistantMessageID":"msg_Y29tcGFyZS4xL2F0dGVtcHQuMi93cml0ZXIuMQ_00000000000000000004","ordinal":0,"sessionID":"ses_Y29tcGFyZS4xL2F0dGVtcHQuMi93cml0ZXIuMQ","text":"**Token caching** (often implemented via KV caching or prompt caching) stores intermediate model computations so repetitive text doesn't need to be reprocessed from scratch.\n\nWhen an LLM processes text, it calculates key-value (KV) representations for every token to evaluate attention. In multi-turn conversations, repeated system prompts, or large document queries, the beginning prefix of the prompt often stays identical across calls. \n\nInstead of recomputing attention states for these identical prefixes on every request, the system saves the KV activations in memory. When a new query arrives with a matching prefix, the model retrieves the cached states and processes only the newly added tokens.\n\nThis yields two significant benefits:\n1. **Lower Latency:** Time-to-first-token (TTFT) drops dramatically because the prefill phase skips already-digested context.\n2. **Reduced Cost:** Providers can charge significantly less for cached prompt tokens, making long-context workflows and persistent system instructions much more economical.\n"}},"native_ref":{"messageID":"8bfb73b0-88b4-477c-9594-b1e13b0a8421/step.1","normalizedMessageID":"msg_Y29tcGFyZS4xL2F0dGVtcHQuMi93cml0ZXIuMQ_00000000000000000004","provider":"agy","sessionID":"8bfb73b0-88b4-477c-9594-b1e13b0a8421"}}
